WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
The second CTE parses dates.

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:06.072
It recognizes two documented formats.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:11.162
If the raw text matches one of them, PostgreSQL creates a typed date.

00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:17.142
If the value does not match a supported format, the clean date becomes NULL.

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:23.282
That preserves the distinction between known and unsupported input.

00:00:23.282 --> 00:00:27.747
Connect this idea to a customer master-data project cleaning duplicate profiles.

00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:33.847
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:43.682
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.682 --> 00:00:49.327
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:49.327 --> 00:00:55.040
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
